// ---------- İngilizce örnekler ----------
// Paragraf düzeni Türkçe kümeyle aynı. Kâğıdın Hafızası'nın çevirisi Kenar Notu'nun kendi metni; Suç ve Ceza ile
// Dönüşüm özgün eserlerden (kamu malı) Kenar Notu için yeniden aktarıldı, yayımlanmış bir çeviriden alınmadı.
const KAGIT_EN = {
  id: "kagit",
  kitaplik: { ad: "The Memory of Paper", yazar: "Essay" },
  baslik: "The Memory of Paper",
  yazar: "Kenar Notu",
  tohumlar: [
    { renk: "gok", metin: "a letter whose reply was long overdue", not: "Read this together with the last paragraph." },
    { renk: "limon", metin: "The margin is the one empty chair the printer leaves for the reader.", not: "The whole essay grows out of this sentence." },
    { renk: "pembe", metin: "Çığ ışığı, İğne, Öğüt, Şükür." },
    { renk: "nane", metin: "the page where the book falls open by itself tells you what its readers needed most." },
  ],
  bolumler: [
    {
      no: 1,
      baslik: "The Voice in the Margin",
      paragraflar: [
        "On page forty-seven of a book I bought from a secondhand bookseller, under a thin line drawn in pencil, I found a sentence someone had written to me. It was not written to me, of course; whoever wrote it did not know me, and may well have written it before I was born. But like every margin note, it spoke to every reader who would ever open the book, and so, a little, to me. “I thought so too, but I was alone,” the line said. Below it was a date, the month written in Roman numerals: 12.III.1987. When I took the book to the counter, the bookseller fanned the yellowed pages with his thumb and named a price. Had he seen the notes, and if he had, did he care? I don’t know. For me, from that moment on, the book stopped being a book; it became a letter, or more precisely, a letter whose reply was long overdue.",
        "The margin is the one empty chair the printer leaves for the reader. The typesetter leaves that whiteness so the eye can rest, the page can breathe and the book can be held in the hand; nobody tells him that a conversation will begin there. Yet the moment readers pick up a pencil, they sit down in that chair. The text has said something to them, and they answer. Sometimes the answer is a single mark: an exclamation point, a question mark, a shaky line running the full length of a sentence. Sometimes it is a half-page objection, sometimes just the word “no,” underlined three times. The margin is where the book’s own voice ends and another voice begins; I know of no other place where two voices stand so close together.",
        "Most of these notes, in time, stop saying anything even to the people who wrote them. Anyone who has opened an old textbook years later knows this: pages painted with highlighter, stars at the start of certain paragraphs, “IMPORTANT” written in capitals in the margin. Why was it important? Which exam, which night, which fear put that star there? We recognize our own handwriting but not our own thinking. A margin note is a voice torn loose from the moment it was written; the context that carried its meaning grows old and leaves along with the person who wrote it. What remains is the mark itself, like a dry leaf: we know it was once alive, but we can no longer say which tree it fell from.",
        "Still, margin notes have a language of their own, and that language changes surprisingly little from one generation to the next. A vertical line means “read this again,” two vertical lines mean “learn this by heart.” A question mark stands for doubt, an exclamation point for surprise, and a square bracket shows where a thought begins and where it ends. Some readers build their own index in the top corner of the page; others write only page numbers and one-word headings in the blank space of the last page: “death, 34; sea, 112; his mother, 140.” When you find an index like that, you have found another book running through the book. The same pages, the same sentences, but a life arranged in an entirely different order.",
        "Writing in library books is forbidden, and the rule is usually a fair one. Even so, the most heavily annotated books are found in libraries. Readers did not write there to catch someone else’s attention; they wrote because they could not help it. A sentence shook them so much that the pencil found its own way to the margin. These notes are usually written very small and very light, as if in apology. Sometimes the next reader adds a reply beside the earlier note, and in the quiet reading rooms a long argument that nobody hears goes on for years.",
        "Textbooks are the most crowded cities of margin notes. A physics book passed from an older brother to a younger one, from one class to the next, holds three pencils, three hands, three fears. One reader wrote little memory aids beside the formulas, another squeezed solutions into the margins, and a third added his own objections on top of it all. The book’s actual author nearly disappears in the crowd. But perhaps this is a textbook at its best: a notebook that grows from hand to hand, showing that learning is the work not of one person but of a long line of people, a whole generation.",
        SAHNE,
        "On the bottom shelf of the cupboard in my grandmother’s house there was a thin alphabet primer. It had no cover; one of its first leaves, clearly opened and closed many times, had split down the middle. The book had belonged to my grandmother’s mother. After the alphabet reform, when Turkish exchanged the Arabic script for Latin letters, she was nearly thirty when she went to the neighborhood evening school and learned the new letters there, a tired but stubborn student. The margins held words in her handwriting. First single letters, then syllables, then words. On one page, beside the lesson on the letter “ç,” four words that had nothing to do with one another stood side by side, their first letters carefully enlarged: “Çığ ışığı, İğne, Öğüt, Şükür.”",
        "As a child I thought these words were a spell. Read aloud, they left a strange taste in the mouth: the chill of the avalanche, the point of the needle, the weight of advice and the soft closing of thanks. Much later I understood that my great-grandmother had chosen them not for their meanings but for their letters. Sounds she had written in quite different shapes in the old script had turned, in the new one, into letters with tails, dots and hooks, and she wanted to see them all together and train her hand on them. The hook of Ç, the little cup over ğ, the missing dot of ı, the extra dot of İ, the two dots of ö and ü looking up like eyes, the curl under ş. These four words, set down in the margin, were a ladder a woman built for herself while she grew used to a new script.",
        "On the last page of the book there was only a name, written in the new letters and plainly written that way for the first time. The letters were not the same size and the line climbed upward, but the name was complete, every dot and hook in its place. I don’t know what it feels like to write your own name in a new script for the first time. Perhaps a little strangeness, and a little of the joy of being born again. Whenever I look at that page, I think that the margin of a book bears witness not only to reading but to learning as well.",
        "Perhaps this is what we mean by the memory of paper: it remembers not what was written but the hand that wrote it. Where a pencil was pressed, the fibers are slightly crushed, and years later, even if the writing has been erased, the dent is still there when you tilt the page toward the light. Blind impressions that went through to the leaf below, the half-moon left by a tea glass, the diagonal crease where a corner was folded and smoothed out again. The book was not written to tell of these things, yet it carries them all. In the wrinkle of a page lies a night when someone fell asleep reading it.",
        "Book conservators know one thing well: paper records, in one way or another, everything that is done to it. Damp makes it ripple, sunlight yellows it, the corners touched by fingers slowly darken. Pages that are read often are softer; hold a book by its spine and let go, and it opens where it has been opened most. This is the most honest confession a book can make. The contents page tells you what the book is about; the page where the book falls open by itself tells you what its readers needed most.",
        "Some readers consider folding a page a kind of savagery; bending a corner when there is a bookmark at hand is, to them, disrespect toward the paper. I have always been undecided about this. A bookmark leaves no trace once it is taken out of the book; a folded corner stays even after it is smoothed flat. Those small overlapping triangles at the edge of a book show where a reader stopped, where they caught their breath, where they left off until the next day. A bookmark marks a place; a fold marks a time.",
        SAHNE,
        "To return to the book I bought from the secondhand bookseller: the person who wrote the notes fell silent around the middle of the book. First the notes grew sparse, then only lines remained, and finally those ended too. After page one hundred and ninety the book looked untouched; even the edges of the leaves were whiter. I thought about that silence for a long time. Had they grown tired of the book? Been caught up in something else? Or had the book given them the answer they were looking for on page one hundred and ninety and set them free? Sometimes what margin notes say most clearly is where they stop. The page where a reader put down the pencil is the most secret sentence in their whole relationship with the book.",
        "Some of the notes were about the book itself: a character’s name, a corrected date, a misprinted letter marked with a thin line. Others were only about the reader. On the page describing a letter from a father to his son, there was a single line in the margin: “My father used to say this too. I didn’t listen.” Right beside it, in the same pencil but clearly on another day, in darker and steadier letters, a second line: “Now I am listening.” How many years lay between the two lines, I don’t know. But those two lines told a longer story than all the chapters of the book.",
        "The handwriting itself told a story too. The letters were small, slanted and leaning on one another; they crowded toward the end of the line, and when the margin ran out they curled down toward the bottom of the page and kept going. This was the handwriting of someone with a great deal to say and nowhere to put it. Some notes had been crossed out and a shorter version written beside them, as if even in the margin the writer had felt the need to correct themselves. In one place there was only a telephone number, shorter than today’s, with no area code. I thought about calling it, then decided against it. Some doors lead somewhere only as long as they stay closed.",
        "On that page, just under the last note, I added a small mark of my own: an arrow. I didn’t quite know what I meant by it either; perhaps “I am here,” perhaps “I am carrying on.” I drew it in pencil so that it could be erased. Then I laughed at my own small timidity. Everyone who writes in a margin is a little brave; but whoever writes in pencil leaves, beside their courage, a door slightly ajar.",
        "They say that reading is being able to be alone in a crowded room. Margin notes correct the saying a little: reading is being in company in a room that looks empty. Others read the lines in front of us before we did; some left their voices behind, most did not. The margin of a book is a small window they left open for us. Looking in, we see no one, but we know the light in the room is on.",
      ],
    },
    {
      no: 2,
      baslik: "The Patience of Ink",
      paragraflar: [
        "Some of the inks used in old manuscripts eventually eat the paper they were written on. This dark ink, made with iron salts and an acid drawn from oak galls, is bright and resolute on the day it is written; but over the centuries it slowly gnaws at the fibers. On some pages the insides of the letters have hollowed out and the writing has opened tiny windows in the paper. Conservators call this ink corrosion. When I first saw it in a museum case, I thought it was writing at its most honest: the words had not been content to sit on the paper, they had worked their way into it and through it. Remembering is sometimes like that; to leave a mark, you wound a little.",
        "In the Ottoman tradition of writing there was another kind of permanence. Notes set down in the margins of manuscripts were called hâşiye, derkenar or şerh; around a text a second text grew, at times more crowded than the main one. A book in the middle of the page, and in its margins the voices of the generations who read it. In some copies the notes run in slanting lines, written so that you have to turn the page to read them; to write down what they had learned, readers had to turn the book in their hands. There the margin was not just an empty space but a gathering for conversation. Under a teacher’s note a student’s was added, under that the note of the student’s student; as the book passed from hand to hand it did not grow thicker, but it grew deeper.",
        "When I first heard the word derkenar, I was charmed by the small translation hidden inside it: in the margin. Only that. This modest word, which says where a note was written, in time became the name of the note itself. Perhaps the best names are born this way, by saying not what a thing is but where it stands. The margin looks like the opposite of the center, but it is in fact the center’s most faithful witness. Everything said at the center waits to find an echo in the margin.",
        "The soot ink of calligraphers is the product of another kind of patience. Lamp soot was mixed with gum and water and pounded for hours, sometimes for days; it was said that ink made in haste spreads on the paper, while ink made with patience keeps its shine for centuries. This ink does not eat the paper; it rests on top of it like a shadow. A wrongly written letter was removed by moistening the surface of the burnished paper or by scraping it away very finely. So the old writing had an eraser as well; only that eraser was sometimes a knife, and every correction left a small hollow in the paper. This is what I mean by the patience of ink: it waits while it is being made, and it does not move once it is written.",
        SAHNE,
        "The modesty of the pencil deserves a mention here too. Graphite does not soak into paper; it rubs off against the fibers and stays there. That is why it can be erased, why it smudges, why it turns faintly silver when the light falls on it. A note written in pencil seems to ask permission: “May I stay here?” Ink does not ask permission; it makes a promise. The line that flows from a fountain pen is absorbed by the paper and remains; if it was written wrongly it is crossed out, but it never quite goes away. That is why writing in someone’s book in ink is like a small vow.",
        "The highlighter is the invention of another age, the age of speed and exams. It stands somewhere between reading and writing: it adds nothing, it only selects. In my student years I had friends who painted whole pages yellow, and we laughed at them; now, thinking back, I see a kind of helplessness in those painted pages. To mark everything is to be unable to let go of anything. To choose is to accept a loss; when we underline one sentence, we also say something to all the sentences we did not underline. A good highlight is therefore a kind of renunciation.",
        "Some readers tie this choosing to a system: yellow for what is important, pink for what is beautiful, green for what to ask about, blue for what links to another book. The meaning of the colors changes from reader to reader, but the need is the same; we want not only to remember what we read but to sort it, to put it somewhere. A colorful page is therefore like a small library catalog. Only in this catalog the shelves are not outside but inside the reader’s mind, and every reader struggles to decode someone else’s colors.",
        "Our first meeting with the margin often happens in the margin of something we wrote ourselves. The red ink of school notebooks: a teacher’s quick, slanted strokes, a “well done,” a “careful,” a circle drawn around a wrong letter. Some of those circles we still remember thirty years later. Perhaps that is why we learn so early how much weight a thing written in the margin carries: the margin is proof that someone read us. Every reader who writes in the margin of a book seems to remember that they, too, were once read; now it is their turn.",
        "Medieval copyists are said to have sometimes written their own troubles in the margins: that their hands were cold, that the light was failing, that the ink was bad, how much was left before the work was done. Someone reading those margins hundreds of years later hears, beside a text held to be sacred, the voice of a person who was cold. These small complaints have always seemed to me the most touching notes of all, because they remind us that a book, too, was made with labor, with a body, in a particular room and in particular weather. Behind every page there is a hand that carried it.",
        SAHNE,
        "Today we write most of our notes on glass. On a screen the tip of the pen meets none of the resistance it meets on paper; but interestingly, the hand still looks for the margin. Even in a digital book, when we want to write something next to a sentence, our eyes drift to that empty strip on the right. The margin is less a property of paper than a habit of reading, perhaps a need. While we read a text we want to talk with it, and the conversation has to be written down somewhere. Glass does not forget, but it does not remember either; it does not remember the way paper does, by bending, by wearing, by yellowing.",
        "Perhaps the task of the digital margin is not to imitate the memory of paper but to protect what paper taught us: that reading is not a one-way act. While a book speaks to us, we can answer it. Our answer can be a color, a circle, an arrow, a question. What matters is to leave a trace that the thing we read passed through us. Books read without leaving a trace leave something too, of course, but it is hard to find out later what they left.",
        "The strangest encounter, though, is with our own notes. When we reopen at forty a book we read at twenty, we find a young stranger in the margins. Where they grew excited, we stay calm; at a sentence they passed without noticing, we stop and pick up our pencil. Two notes come to stand side by side, and two different times of the same person begin to argue. The book has not changed; what has changed is the hand that holds it. That is why margin notes are a map not only of the book but of the reader.",
        "There is one more kind of note often found on the first pages of old books: the ownership inscription. Ottoman readers called it temellük kaydı. It begins “This book belongs to,” and sometimes ends with a date, sometimes with a prayer, sometimes with a gentle warning to those who borrow books and never return them. These inscriptions pile up at the top of the page; each new owner adds a name beneath the one before. The first page of a book thus turns into a family tree. A book really belongs to no one; it only stays in someone’s hands for a while, then goes on its way.",
        "The first page of a book given as a gift is a different margin altogether. There you find a name, a date, and often a hurried, slightly clumsy sentence: “Happy birthday,” “Safe travels,” “Think of me when you read this.” As the book changes hands this page stays in place, and on secondhand shelves the loving sentences of people who never knew one another line up in silence. Wandering between the shelves, I like to open the cover of a random book and look at that first page. Most of the time it is blank. But when it is not, I come upon one of the warmest sentences in a stranger’s life at a moment I least expect it.",
        SAHNE,
        "I finished the book from the secondhand bookseller last winter. From page one hundred and ninety onward I read it inside the silence that stranger had left, a little on their behalf. I added my own notes, in pencil and in small letters, not beside theirs but below them. As if, sitting at the same table, I did not want to interrupt. When I reached the last page I wrote a date in the blank space inside the back cover. Then, after a long hesitation, I took the book back to the shop where I had bought it.",
        "I didn’t tell anyone what I wrote. Even if I had, it would not matter; margin notes fade when they are repeated anywhere else, and they carry meaning only on the page they belong to, beside the sentence they belong to. Separating a note from its book is like pulling a leaf from its branch: its shape stays the same, but it no longer grows. That is why I never liked copying my notes into a notebook. Leaving them in the book meant trusting that the book would keep them better than I could.",
        "The bookseller took the book, fanned its pages with his thumb again, and this time stopped somewhere. He had seen my arrow, perhaps the date under it too. He said nothing; he only smiled faintly and put the book back on the shelf, in its old place. Now it is waiting there for another reader. One day that reader will open page forty-seven and see two different hands, two different times, standing side by side under the same sentence. Perhaps they will add something too. Perhaps they will add nothing, but as they close the book they will think for a moment of those who read it before them.",
        "In the end the memory of paper outlasts ours. We forget: what we marked and why, on which night our eyes filled at which sentence, whose book we lent to whom. Paper does not forget, but it does not keep what it remembers to itself either; it tells the next reader, quietly, from the margins. Perhaps this is what reading is most for: letting people who never saw one another meet on the same page across time. Every note you write in a margin is a letter to someone you have not met yet.",
      ],
    },
  ],
};

