        "“I knew it!” he muttered anxiously. “I thought so! That is the worst of all! A trivial detail like this could spoil the whole plan. This hat is too noticeable; it looks ridiculous, and that is exactly why it draws attention. Nobody wears anything like it; it can be spotted a verst away and remembered. Being remembered is the real problem. One must not attract attention. Details, the details are what matter most!”",
        "He had not far to go; he even knew how many steps it was from the gate of his house: exactly seven hundred and thirty. He had counted them once, when he was lost in his daydreams. Back then he had not believed in those dreams himself; he had only teased himself with their ugly but tempting boldness. Now, a month later, he had begun to see them differently. In spite of all his inner speeches about his own lack of nerve, he had grown used, almost against his will, to thinking of this ugly dream as an actual undertaking. Now he was going to rehearse it, and his excitement grew with every step.",
        "His heart was pounding when he reached an enormous building with one side on the canal and the other on the street. The building was divided into small flats from top to bottom; tailors, locksmiths, cooks, girls living on their own and minor clerks lived there. He went into the courtyard and turned to the dark, narrow staircase on the right. He even liked the darkness: in such darkness even the most curious eyes were no danger. On his way up to the fourth floor he came upon porters carrying furniture out of a flat; the clerk who lived there was moving out. “So for a while only the old woman’s flat on this landing will be occupied,” he thought. Then he rang the bell. It gave a feeble tinkle, as if it were made of tin rather than copper.",
        "The door opened a tiny crack. The woman inside looked at the visitor with open suspicion, her small eyes glinting out of the dark; seeing other people on the landing, she took courage and opened the door wide. She was a tiny, dried-up old woman of about sixty, with sharp, spiteful eyes and a small pointed nose. Her head was bare; her graying hair was slick with oil. She had wound a strip of flannel around her long thin neck, and despite the heat a shabby fur cape, losing its fur, hung from her shoulders. She coughed and groaned all the time.",
        "“Raskolnikov, a student. I was here a month ago,” the young man muttered, bowing slightly as he remembered that he ought to be polite. “I remember, sir, I remember very well that you came,” said Alyona Ivanovna, not taking her suspicious eyes off his face. Then, with the same hesitation, she stepped aside to let him in.",
        "The little room had yellow wallpaper, geraniums and muslin curtains at the windows, and at that moment it was lit by the setting sun. “So the sun will shine like this then, too,” flashed through his mind. He glanced quickly around the room, as if he wanted to fix everything in it in his memory. The furniture was old and made of yellow wood: a huge sofa with a curved back, an oval table in front of it, a few chairs along the wall, a dressing table with a mirror, a few cheap pictures. In the corner a lamp burned before an icon. Everything was spotless. “Lizaveta’s work,” he thought.",
        "“What do you want?” the old woman said sharply. “I have brought something to pawn,” said the young man, and took from his pocket an old flat silver watch; a globe was engraved on its back, and its chain was steel. The woman reminded him that the time on his earlier pledge had run out the day before yesterday. The young man said he would bring the month’s interest as well. She turned the watch over and over, and at last said she could give a ruble and a half, with the interest taken in advance. “A ruble and a half! It was my father’s.” But they both knew he had nowhere else to go.",
        "The woman took out her keys and went into the other room behind the curtain. Left alone, the young man listened hard: he heard a chest of drawers being opened. “The top drawer, then,” he thought. “She carries the keys in her right pocket, all on a steel ring.” When she came back she did the sums: once the interest was taken off, he got one ruble and fifteen kopecks. The young man took the money and turned to the door, then, as if in passing, asked whether her sister Lizaveta was at home and said he might bring something else in a few days.",
