        "There were few people in the tavern at that hour. Raskolnikov’s attention was caught by a man who looked like a retired clerk. He was past fifty, of medium height, stocky, gray-haired and balding on top. His face, swollen from constant drinking, was yellowish, even greenish; between his puffy eyelids his small reddened eyes glittered. But there was something strange in those eyes: an eagerness, perhaps a spark of intelligence, and at the same time something like madness. The man had been watching Raskolnikov for some time; at last he got up and came over to his table.",
        "“My dear sir,” he began with a dignified air, “may I be so bold as to start a conversation with you? Your clothes are not very grand, but my experienced eye sees in you an educated man, one who is not used to drink. I have always respected learning joined with the feelings of the heart. My name is Marmeladov; I am a clerk, or rather, I was a clerk.”",
        "“Poverty is no vice, sir; that is a true saying. I know that drunkenness is no virtue either, and I know it better than anyone. But destitution, my dear sir, destitution is a vice. In poverty a man still keeps the natural refinement of his feelings; in destitution no one can. A destitute man is not even driven out with a stick but swept out with a broom, so that it will be all the more humiliating. Do you know, sir, what it means to have nowhere left to go? Every man must have at least one place he can go.”",
