        "Most of all he spoke of his daughter from his first marriage, Sonya. One evening when the family had nothing to eat, after something her stepmother said in anger, Sonya got up at six o’clock, tied on her kerchief, put on her cape and went out; she came back at nine. Without a word she laid thirty rubles on the table, pulled her big green shawl over her head and lay down on the bed with her face to the wall; only her shoulders were trembling. That night Katerina Ivanovna, without a word, knelt at her feet.",
        "Marmeladov’s voice shook as he told this. Not long ago he had found a new post; his wife had been beside herself with joy, made him coffee, mended his old clothes. But he had secretly taken his first wages from the house and had been drinking for five days. Today he had gone to his daughter and asked her for money for drink. “She gave me thirty kopecks, with her own hand, her last. She said nothing, she only looked at me in silence.” His face showed shame and, at the same time, something anguished, almost rapturous; as he told it, he seemed to be punishing himself.",
        "“There is no one to pity me, is there? There is nothing in me to pity! But He who pities everyone and understands everything will pity us. That day will come and He will ask: where is the daughter who gave herself up for her bad-tempered, consumptive stepmother and for another woman’s little children? And He will forgive her. Then He will call to us too: come forth, you drunkards, come forth, you weaklings! And we shall all come forth and stand before Him, ashamed.”",
        "When they left the tavern, Marmeladov could hardly stand. Raskolnikov took him all the way home. In the narrow, smoky room at the top of the stairs, Katerina Ivanovna was pacing from corner to corner with her hands pressed to her chest; one of the children was crying. When she saw her husband she seized him by the hair and dragged him into the room. Marmeladov, on his knees, was crying out with meek delight: “This is not pain to me, my dear sir, this is a pleasure!”",
        "Raskolnikov left without a word. On his way out he put his hand in his pocket and quietly left on the windowsill the coins it found there, the change from the tavern. On the stairs he regretted it and almost turned back. “What a stupid thing I have done,” he thought. “They have their Sonya, and I don’t have enough even for myself.” Then he smiled bitterly: “Man gets used to everything. Everything.”",
