        "He woke late the next morning. His sleep had been troubled and had not rested him. He looked around his room with anger. It was a tiny cell six steps long; yellow, dusty paper hung from the walls, and the ceiling was so low that anyone a little tall would feel uneasy there, as if about to bump his head at any moment. The furniture suited the room: three old chairs, a painted table with a few dusty notebooks and books on it, and a large, shapeless sofa that took up half the room. Raskolnikov often slept on it without undressing, his old student coat pulled over him.",
        "Nastasya, the landlady’s servant, brought him tea and some of the cabbage soup she had set aside from her own share. She told him the landlady meant to complain to the police. Then she took an envelope from her pocket: “Oh, and a letter came for you yesterday.” Raskolnikov turned pale. The letter was from his mother. He waited for Nastasya to leave, raised the letter to his lips and kissed it, then looked for a long time at the familiar, small, slanting handwriting on the envelope.",
        "His mother, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, had written at great length. First she apologized for the months she had been unable to send him money, then she told him what had happened to his sister Dunya. While Dunya was working as a governess in the Svidrigailovs’ house, the master of the house had made improper advances to her; his wife, Marfa Petrovna, misunderstanding the situation, had put all the blame on Dunya and turned her out of the house in front of everyone. For days the whole town buzzed with the gossip, and no one would even greet them.",
        "“But, my dear Rodya, our trial did not last long, and my heart is full of gratitude to God,” his mother wrote. Svidrigailov had been forced to give his wife a letter showing how Dunya had refused him; Marfa Petrovna had then come in tears to beg Dunya’s pardon, and afterward went from door to door through the town, telling everyone what a pure girl she was.",
